  war  i fear war already marches on their own  lands  this is a reference to an incident that  only appears in the appendices to the  lord of the rings the battle of dale  this monumentally important conflict  takes place concurrently with the siege  of ministerith and the battle of the  pelanor fields the two-week-long  confrontation finds an army of men and  dwarves led by king brand of dale and  dane ironfoot fighting against sauron's  easterlings in the course of the battle  both brand and dane are killed but their  successors managed to prevent the  easterlings from breaking through  towards gondor after the ring is  destroyed they finally drive the enemy  from their lands both dale and erebor  have already been depicted in live  action in all three hobbit movies but  the battle of dale has yet to warrant  anything more than a brief mention in a  single deleted scene still this savage  confrontation is rife with drama from  brand and dane's last stand at the gate  of airbor to the darkest hours of the  easterling siege to the final push to  free the mountain and its people newline  cinema is currently gearing up to  release an anime feature film about the  life of helm hammerhead the legendary  king of rohan and if the studio is  indeed eyeing an anime-based future for  their middle-earth stories they could do  much worse than an adaptation of the  battle of dale ask any token fan for the  one chapter in middle earth's history  they'd like to see adapted into live  action and there's a pretty solid chance  they'll say  the angmar war again this story appears  almost solely within the appendices to  the lord of the rings this war is fought  between the forces of agmar led by the  witch king and the manish kingdom of  arnor and it takes place over the course  of some 600 years during the mid-third  age the conflict engulfs the region of  eriador in northwestern middle earth  bringing together men and elves from  every corner of the world to stand  against sauron's chief servant but  before the end of the war arnor itself  has been almost entirely annihilated  though the free peoples do eventually  defeat the witch king and force him to  flee to parts unknown in fact it's after  the final great confrontation of the war  the battle of the fornost that the elf  lord glorfendell infamously prophesizes  that no man will kill the witch king and  you probably know how that turns out  i am no man  [Music]  the angmar war is one of the darkest and  most brutal conflicts in the history of  the third age while this narrative is  marked by death disease and destruction  it also exhibits the same sense of hope  and unity that the lord of the rings  does so well  obviously no single movie could possibly  capture the sheer scale of the witch  king's finest hour but a long form  series would be another matter entirely  otherwise known as the asari the five  angelic mayar who come to middle-earth  as wizards play extraordinarily  important roles in the fight against  sauron these five wizards are saruman  the white gandalf the gray radagast the  brown and the two blue wizards alatar  and palundo you probably know how things  play out with saruman and gandalf the  former allows his ambition to get the  better of him falling into darkness  before his own lackey unceremoniously  assassinates him the latter well is  gandalf isn't he you  shall not  pass  ratagas meanwhile settles down near  mirkwood for much of the third age  befriending the birds and beasts of the  forest and keeping an eye on the growing  shadow in dolgodur in the books he  inadvertently leads gandalf in saruman's  trap before helping him escape and then  pretty much vanishes from middle-earth  altogether the blue wizards venture  eastwards to either disrupt sauron's  plans or establish their own magical  cults depending on which of tolkien's  writings you take as gospel saruman  gandolf and radogast arrive in middle  earth around the year 1000 of the third  age while the blue wizards possibly show  up sometime in the second age this means  that the astari have been roaming for at  least 2 000 years before frodo embarks  on his quest thanks to tolkien's  reticence on their history readers have  only their imaginations to suggest what  they might have been doing during that  time perhaps a spin-off of some kind  following the wizards on their  adventures in the mortal world could  shed some light on the matter the story  of the kingdom of kazadoom is long  glorious and  dark  literally best known as the setting for  some of the most iconic moments in the  fellowship of the ring moria once housed  a thriving dwarvish realm that stretched  far beneath the deep reaches of the  misty mountains during the first and  second ages of middle earth the history  of kazudum is one of peace prosperity  and expansion even during the turbulent  days of the late second age these  dwarves avert the ravages of war by  shutting their gates and cutting  themselves off from the outside world  everything changes however during the  third age in the year ta 1980 the  dwarves obsessively mining the stores of  mithril contained within the mountain  accidentally awaken a balrog  shadow  and flame  king duran vi is killed during the  demon's rampage earning it the name  durian's bane and the surviving dwarves  flee their once great kingdom the  dwarves attempt to retake moria a couple  of times over the next few hundred years  but it isn't until the coming of during  the seventh over 170 years after the  events of the lord of the rings that  they finally succeed  the rise fall and rise again of the  greatest dwarvish kingdom is a tragic  and terrifying saga but one that also  goes overlooked by many of tolkien's  readers  no doubt it would provide ample  opportunity for a filmmaker to explore a  darker more horrific side of the  author's world and give the dwarves of  middle-earth some much-needed attention  in the process  the fall of moria briefly intertwines  with another stage in the long journey  of tolkien's dwarves one that would be  equally gripping as its own adaptation  fans have actually glimpsed a  live-action portrayal of the wars of the  dwarves and orcs before the climactic  battle of azernold tsar appears in a  flashback in the hobbit an unexpected  journey but there's a whole lot more to  this desperate struggle between dwarf  and dork the war begins when moria's orc  chieftain azod kills thor the exiled  king under the mountain thrower's son  thrain marches toward to avenge his  fallen father amassing a great army of  dwarves from all across middle earth the  dwarves blunder and pillaged down the  breath of the misty mountains tolkien  writes  both sides were pitiless and there was  death and cruel deeds by dark and by  light after six years of warfare much of  which takes place underground the two  sides clash outside the east gate of  morya in what is later known as the  battle of azinul bazar here dane  ironfoot kills azog and the orcs are  routed though they stop short of  entering moria for fear of joran's bane  worse still the vengeance of the dwarves  comes at a terrible cost and countless  dwarvish lives are lost during this  final battle while the war of the  dwarves and dorics might only constitute  one part of a wider moria project it  could easily stand as its own story and  particularly one for fans of the hobbit  movies a spin-off film or series set  within the boundaries of the shire could  take almost any form it might offer a  pleasant and comedic exploration of the  lives of hobbits during peace time the  stakes needn't be higher than a drunken  expedition to farmer maggot's crop or a  dispute over an elderly relative's  inheritance it could go a little darker  too perhaps bringing the scouring of the  shire to the big screen for the first  time to really show the hobbits in a  brand new light however you'd have to  turn to the war between the hobbits and  orcs that ends with the battle of  greenfields a few hundred years before  the lord of the rings bands of orcs from  the misty mountains invade eriador while  the dunadine rangers managed to keep  most of them at bay one group led by the  goblin king golfenball successfully  enters the shire led by bando brass took  the hobbits meet the orcs in a battle at  the greenfields when took a decapitates  golfing bull and sends his head flying  into a nearby rabbit hole the battle is  won in the game of golf invented at the  same time  in so many of tolkien's stories war is  shown to be very much the province of  men elves dwarves and orcs a short  series animation or even a film that  sees vanderbrass and his hobbits rise up  to defend their homeland would shine a  whole new light on the shire and its  inhabitants  during his lifetime j.r tolkien composed  three stories set during the quentus and  marillion that were written to be  enjoyed as complete works in and of  themselves found in the silmarillion  these stories did not require the reader  to have an understanding of the wider  mythos they are known as the three great  tales of the elder days of middle earth  and by far the most famous of them all  is the tale of baron and luthien  otherwise known as the quest for the  silmaril the narrative revolves around  the forbidden romance between baron a  man and luthien the daughter of the elf  king thingol when their love is exposed  an enraged thingal jokingly promises  that baron will be allowed to marry  luthien if he enters morgoth's fortress  of angband plucks a sacred jewel from  his crown and brings it back to the  elves of doriath to his surprise baron  agrees quickly setting out to fulfill  his task the story was very dear to  tolkien's heart on his gravestone he is  named as baron while his beloved wife  edith is named as luthien perhaps that's  why the tale of baron and luthien  arguably feels like the author's finest  work swathed in the near palpable  passion and devotion he clearly felt  towards his world what happened to her  she died  certainly the lay of lathian an  incomplete poetic rendition of the tale  contains some of his most beautiful and  affecting writing no part of tolkien's  vast legendarium is more deserving of a  faithful adaptation  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

